### Can a sealed class list itself in permits?

No. The `permits` clause declares the set of direct subtypes, and by definition a subtype is a different type that inherits from the sealed type. A class cannot extend itself. Writing `sealed class Account permits Account {}` produces a compile error.

```java
package com.bank.model;

// COMPILE ERROR: cyclic or invalid permitted subtype
// public sealed class Account permits Account {}

// Correct: list different subtypes
public sealed class Account permits SavingsAccount, CurrentAccount {
    String id;
}

final class SavingsAccount extends Account {}
final class CurrentAccount extends Account {}
```

### Is a concrete sealed class the same as self-permitting?

No. A non-abstract sealed class can be instantiated directly, so it is itself a usable leaf value in addition to having permitted subclasses. But it did not permit itself: it permitted other types, and it is usable on its own because it is non-abstract.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Concrete sealed class: usable as a value AND has subtypes
public sealed class LedgerNode permits SummaryNode {
    private final BigDecimal amount;

    public LedgerNode(BigDecimal amount) {
        this.amount = amount;
    }

    public BigDecimal amount() { return amount; }
}

final class SummaryNode extends LedgerNode {
    public SummaryNode(BigDecimal amount) {
        super(amount);
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.LedgerNode;
import com.bank.model.SummaryNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class LedgerDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // LedgerNode is concrete: can instantiate directly
        LedgerNode node = new LedgerNode(new BigDecimal("50000"));
        SummaryNode summary = new SummaryNode(new BigDecimal("150000"));

        System.out.println("Node amount:    " + node.amount());
        System.out.println("Summary amount: " + summary.amount());
    }
}
```

```text
Node amount:    50000
Summary amount: 150000
```

### How is a recursive data structure different?

A recursive data structure where a node contains references to other nodes of the same sealed type is completely legal. Recursion in the data (a node holding child nodes) is composition, not an inheritance cycle.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public sealed interface TransactionNode
        permits LeafTransaction, GroupTransaction {
    BigDecimal total();
}

record LeafTransaction(String id, BigDecimal amount)
        implements TransactionNode {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal total() { return amount; }
}

// Recursive: holds a list of TransactionNode children
record GroupTransaction(String label, List<TransactionNode> children)
        implements TransactionNode {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal total() {
        return children.stream()
                .map(TransactionNode::total)
                .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class RecursiveDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TransactionNode tree = new GroupTransaction("Batch", List.of(
                new LeafTransaction("T1", new BigDecimal("25000")),
                new LeafTransaction("T2", new BigDecimal("75000")),
                new GroupTransaction("Sub-batch", List.of(
                        new LeafTransaction("T3", new BigDecimal("50000"))
                ))
        ));

        System.out.println("Batch total: " + tree.total() + " INR");
    }
}
```

```text
Batch total: 150000 INR
```

### Why does Java forbid inheritance cycles?

Java's inheritance forms a directed acyclic graph rooted at `Object`. Any cycle, including a one-node self-cycle, is forbidden throughout Java, not just for sealed types. The `permits` clause inherits this fundamental constraint.

```java
// None of these compile:
// class A extends A { }           // self-cycle
// class A extends B { } class B extends A { }  // two-node cycle
// sealed class A permits A { }    // self-permit = self-cycle
```

### How is the rule recorded in the class file?

The permitted set goes into the `PermittedSubclasses` class-file attribute, listing the direct subclasses. The sealed class's own entry is never in that list because the attribute describes who may extend it, not the class itself. The JVM validates at load time.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class PermittedCheck {
    sealed interface Account permits Savings, Current {}
    final record Savings() implements Account {}
    final record Current() implements Account {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Class<?>[] permitted = Account.class.getPermittedSubclasses();
        boolean containsSelf = Arrays.asList(permitted).contains(Account.class);
        System.out.println("Account in its own permits: " + containsSelf);
    }
}
```

```text
Account in its own permits: false
```

### What are the design alternatives?

If you want the sealed type to be usable as a value and also closed, make it a non-abstract sealed class with permitted subtypes. If you want recursive data, use a field of the sealed type. Neither involves self-permitting.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Design alternative 1: concrete sealed base
public sealed class FeeRule permits DiscountedFeeRule {
    protected BigDecimal rate;

    public FeeRule(BigDecimal rate) {
        this.rate = rate;
    }

    public BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal principal) {
        return principal.multiply(rate);
    }
}

final class DiscountedFeeRule extends FeeRule {
    private final BigDecimal discount;

    DiscountedFeeRule(BigDecimal rate, BigDecimal discount) {
        super(rate);
        this.discount = discount;
    }

    @Override
    public BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal principal) {
        return super.calculate(principal).subtract(discount);
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes with sealed class self-permitting?

The most common mistake is assuming that a concrete sealed class with no permits clause is extensible. It is not: a sealed class with no `permits` implicitly permits only classes in the same compilation unit, and if none exist, it is effectively final. Another error is confusing a sealed class permitting itself (which Java forbids) with a sealed class that permits its own nested classes, which is the standard and correct approach.

```java
package com.bank.model;

public class SealedSelfPermitMistakes {
    // WRONG assumption: concrete sealed class is extensible
    // sealed class Account { } // No permits, no subclasses in file -> effectively final

    // CORRECT: sealed class permits nested subclasses
    sealed static class Account permits Account.Savings, Account.Current {
        static final class Savings extends Account { }
        static final class Current extends Account { }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Sealed with no permits + no subclasses = effectively final");
        System.out.println("Sealed permits Nested.Savings, Nested.Current = standard pattern");
        System.out.println("Sealed permits itself = compile error (inheritance cycle)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Sealed with no permits + no subclasses = effectively final
Sealed permits Nested.Savings, Nested.Current = standard pattern
Sealed permits itself = compile error (inheritance cycle)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Recognizing the distinction between "a concrete sealed base" and "a recursive field" prevents incorrect designs. If you find yourself wanting a type to permit itself, step back and ask: do I want the base to be a usable value (make it concrete), or do I want a tree structure (add a field of the sealed type)? Both are clean solutions that do not require self-permitting.
